// toolbox/osutils.cla: curated OS Utilities / Gestalt declarations
// (Inside Macintosh II "OS Utilities"; Gestalt from IM VI ch3).
//
// Provenance: Gestalt proven verbatim (including the d0/a1 register
// binding) against runtime/clarus/ui.cla's UiGestalt and
// testsuite/toolbox/cases_gestalt.cla's TbGestalt, both real
// (0xA1AD, reg(d0: selector, a1: response) ret d0) traps. NOTE a real
// discrepancy, not agreement: Retro68 multiversal/defs/Gestalt.yaml
// claims `responsep` is `register: Out<A0>`, but that is WRONG for
// actual 68k hardware -- Gestalt.h's own `#pragma parameter __D0
// Gestalt(__D0, __A1)` (A1, not A0) is the real convention, and
// ui.cla:285-300 documents the concrete failure this caused: declaring
// Gestalt as plain pascal (pre-Task-12) left cg68k pushing args on the
// stack instead of loading D0/A1, so the register-based trap read a
// garbage selector and wrote through a wild A0 pointer, hanging the
// machine on a real native boot -- fixed by switching to `reg(d0:
// selector, a1: response)`, i.e. A1, which is what this catalog uses and
// what the empirical boot (not the multiversal defs) confirms is
// correct. This is the reason multiversal is treated as UNTRUSTED WHEN
// CITED ALONE file-wide in this catalog (see below): it already produced
// one wrong register binding, so every other multiversal-only citation
// here has since been re-checked directly against Apple's own Universal
// Interfaces 3.4 (Retro68/InterfacesAndLibraries/Interfaces), the
// authoritative source, and is superseded by that re-check.
//
//   Gestalt  Directly re-confirmed (toolbox-cookbook phase, 2026-08-07)
//            against CIncludes/Gestalt.h:64 `#pragma parameter __D0
//            Gestalt(__D0, __A1)` and AIncludes/Gestalt.a:43 `_Gestalt:
//            OPWORD $A1AD` -- selector in D0, response in A1, OSErr
//            result in D0: matches `reg(d0: selector, a1: response) ret
//            d0` exactly, and directly refutes multiversal's `Out<A0>`.
//
// SysBeep proven verbatim against runtime/clarus/ui.cla's UiSysBeep
// (0xA9C8); re-confirmed directly against CIncludes/Sound.h:1487
// `SysBeep(short duration) ONEWORDINLINE(0xA9C8)` (SysBeep moved out of
// OSUtils.h into Sound.h in this SDK revision, per that header's own
// comment) and AIncludes/Sound.a:1156 OPWORD $A9C8 -- no register
// pragma, bit 11 set (0xA9C8 & 0x0800 != 0): plain pascal, no `reg`,
// matches.
//
// Delay (0xA03B) was originally checked only against Retro68
// multiversal/defs/OSUtil.yaml (`n` register A0 in, `ftp` register
// `Out<D0>`). Directly re-confirmed against CIncludes/OSUtils.h:352-358
// and AIncludes/OSUtils.a:320, with one correction to the story
// multiversal told: Apple's real signature is TWO parameters, `Delay
// (unsigned long numTicks, unsigned long *finalTicks)`, pragma
// `Delay(__A0, __A1)` (note: no leading `__D0` -- the Pascal-level call
// is a void PROCEDURE, not a function), glued as `TWOWORDINLINE(0xA03B,
// 0x2280)`. Decoding that second word: `0x2280` is the raw 68k
// instruction `MOVE.L D0,(A1)` -- ordinary compiler-emitted glue that
// runs AFTER the trap returns, storing D0 through the caller-supplied
// `finalTicks` pointer in A1. That proves the raw ROM trap itself, before
// this glue instruction executes, already leaves the final tick count in
// D0 and never reads A1 -- A1/finalTicks exists only to satisfy the
// Pascal VAR-parameter ABI at the call site, not the trap's own
// contract. This catalog's declaration below (one parameter, `ret d0`,
// no A1 binding at all) is therefore a faithful, if reduced,
// transcription: it reads the exact same D0 value Apple's glue would
// also copy into `*finalTicks`, just without bothering to also write it
// through a second pointer this codebase never needed. numTicks-in-A0
// matches on both accounts.

const gestaltSystemVersion: int = 0x73797376

external func Gestalt(selector: int, response: ptr): word = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0: selector, a1: response) ret d0
external func Delay(numTicks: int): int = trap 0xA03B reg(a0: numTicks) ret d0
external func SysBeep(duration: word) = trap 0xA9C8
